 ## Overview
-Dual Isosurfacing with Analytical Trilinear Intersection extracts the topology of an analytical isosurface defined by a trilinear function within a voxel cell by determining the optimal set of diagonals to intersect with rays, ensuring one point per disjoint surface component [S1][S2]. Unlike standard Marching Cubes which uses linear interpolation and fixed lookup tables, this method uses the trilinear function exhaustively to determine the topology of the analytical isosurface, allowing for more accurate diagonal selection [S1][S2]. It builds on [Manifold Dual Marching Cubes](2135-manifold-dual-marching-cubes.md) by resolving ambiguous configurations through analytical ray intersection rather than precomputed lookup tables.
